From 9b9a15cd4fc1f293786ca120c4c79f616b001b99 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alex Gorbachenko <alex.gorbachenko@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 31 May 2026 08:33:37 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Replace mpsc channel with TrafficBuffer in encrypted reader
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Replaces the mpsc channel between background reader and read_from()
with a lightweight TrafficBuffer (VecDeque + tokio::sync::Mutex +
Notify).

Background:
  The inbound packet path was:
    peer_reader → router → encrypted_reader_loop → recv_tx →
    recv_rx → Core::read_from → rwc.read → tun_write_loop

  The third hop (encrypted_reader_loop → tun_write_loop via mpsc)
  is a context switch that adds latency on single-core or pinned
  deployments.

Fix:
  1. TrafficBuffer replaces mpsc sender/receiver. read_from() has
     a fast path (pop from buffer) and a slow path (read+decrypt
     inline from inner.read_from() — zero extra CS on the hot path
     when the TUN writer is the one waiting).

  2. A background session_handler_loop still reads from inner to
     process session handshake messages (init/ack), ensuring
     write_to-only nodes complete the handshake.

  3. inner_lock (Arc<Mutex<()>>) serialises access to
     inner.read_from() between the background reader and the
     inline read_from().

At 40 Mbps on hAP ax³ (+jemalloc +cortex-a53 +cpu3 pinning):
  Before (mpsc):        30% CPU
  After  (+TrafficBuf): ~27% CPU  (-1 CS/inbound packet)

The optimisation is universal — any async runtime pays for
cross-task wakeups.  On x86 with larger caches the percentage
improvement is smaller, but the saved context switches are
identical.
